`,s.jsx(F,{items:[{q:"State the three conditions for weak stationarity and name a series that fails each one.",a:"Constant mean — a trending stock index fails. Constant variance — a series whose swings widen every year fails. Autocovariance depending only on lag — a series whose correlation structure changes regime between decades fails."},{q:"ADF returns p = 0.30. What is the conclusion, and what is the trap?",a:"Fail to reject the null, so treat the series as non-stationary and try differencing. The trap is reading a high p-value as 'good' out of habit — ADF's null IS non-stationarity, so failing to reject means you have a problem, not a clean bill of health."},{q:"Match symptom to fix: upward trend / repeating annual pattern / swings that grow with the level.",a:"Trend → first differencing, y_t minus y_{t-1}. Repeating annual pattern → seasonal differencing, y_t minus y_{t-m}. Growing swings → log or Box-Cox transform, usually applied BEFORE differencing since it changes the scale the differencing operates on."},{q:"Why is overdifferencing a real cost rather than harmless insurance?",a:"Each difference removes signal along with the trend and injects extra noise into the series, inflating the variance and introducing a spurious negative autocorrelation at lag 1. That corrupts the ACF/PACF you then use to choose the model order. Stop at the smallest d that passes ADF."}]}),`
